OPERATIONS: ONE DECISION, ONE FIELD
layouts is the FINAL reading order. Each entry names an original layout_id, optionally source_range, and exactly one destination. Reorder entries, not original source text. No page_index, outgoing connection, numerical note index or second ordering field is returned.

Destinations:
- {"kind":"body"}: ordinary main content or an existing main image/table/formula. Ordinary text defaults to a new body paragraph. heading_level is present only at the start of a heading. Joined pieces inherit that paragraph descriptor; do not repeat it. join=true continues the preceding paragraph IN THIS STREAM; on its first text it appends to the preceding-page body cursor. No available cursor means retain an unbound beginning, not discard it.
- {"kind":"note","note_id":"n"}: a member of note n, including diagrams/assets in that note. join is a PARAGRAPH continuation, independent of whether the whole note continues a preceding page. heading_level is optional as for body.
- {"kind":"asset","asset_id":"layout-ID","slot":"title"|"caption"}: only a text part assigned to a retained local image/table's display field, never the owning image/table itself. Assign that owning asset body or note. Its owning body/note is inherited from that asset, not submitted twice. A table may also use slot="content" for its structured content. An image's non-display content is not editable display text. Multiple sources, including an existing Figure caption and a separate "Note: ...", may share the same caption slot. Saying "caption" in reading does not perform this operation.
- {"kind":"independent","stream_id":"d"}: independent description with no reliable asset target. Parts sharing d have their own local paragraph order. Do not force them into main prose or invent a target. Asset fields and independent descriptions begin/end on this page and cannot join other pages.
- {"kind":"ignored"}: explicitly account for a source/range without outputting it. No paragraph, heading, crop or note fields apply. Use for genuine running furniture/printer instructions, excluded notes or duplicate OCR content you have identified; never discard distinct wording that belongs in the requested output. Manuscript/sheet/folio annotations in numbered apparatus are real notes, not disposable furniture.

Only retained body/note destinations may use as_image=true to retain genuinely NON-LINEAR OCR TEXT as a crop. This is not needed for an existing image and is not a way to avoid prose or inline formulas. A graphic in an excluded note is simply ignored. Retained assets themselves have join=false; display formulas break paragraphs, while images/tables can occur inside a paragraph.

An OCR block classified as formula can be corrected to ordinary text by setting as_text=true on a layout entry for that source. Its existing literal content becomes editable text with the same bbox; this applies to all ranges of that source. Use join normally for its final paragraph connections. Omit/false retains the original display-formula representation. This does not read letters from an image or table.

A source can hold several notes or both caption/body. Repeat layout_id with disjoint source_range entries. start/end are source_start/source_end or {"left":"literal text immediately BEFORE","right":"literal text immediately AFTER"}. The quotes must touch in the original source; two phrases merely occurring somewhere are not a boundary. Use enough context for uniqueness; use the identical cut at the end/start of adjacent ranges. All ranges, including ignored ones, form a disjoint complete partition. OCR formula and rich-text wrappers are fallible: source_range may split their literal content. Do not slice/duplicate images or tables or invent sub-boxes.

DUPLICATE OCR: CHOOSE THE RETAINED OCCURRENCE BEFORE SPLITTING
Coverage means every source has a declared destination, not that every copy must be printed. Compare full runs yourself; mere topic similarity is not duplication. When a mixed large box and standalone note boxes contain exactly the same note wording, prefer the complete standalone occurrences, and ignore ALL corresponding repeated ranges inside the large box. In reading briefly name which source supplies each retained run and which supplies only ignored duplicates. Check that the actual destinations implement that choice.

Worked example: A="Body ends here. Continued note text. 8 New note text.", B="Continued note text.", C="8 New note text.". The complete layout plan is:
{"layouts":[
 {"layout_id":"A","source_range":{"start":"source_start","end":{"left":"Body ends here.","right":" Continued note text."}},"destination":{"kind":"body"}},
 {"layout_id":"A","source_range":{"start":{"left":"Body ends here.","right":" Continued note text."},"end":"source_end"},"destination":{"kind":"ignored"}},
 {"layout_id":"B","destination":{"kind":"note","note_id":"tail","join":true}},
 {"layout_id":"C","destination":{"kind":"note","note_id":"new"}}
]}
Do NOT add a retained note range for "Continued note text." from A while also retaining B. Both would target the same note, but still duplicate its text. Ignoring only the numbered final part of A is insufficient. Exclude the ENTIRE repeated suffix beginning at its first duplicated word. Never discard distinct wording. If a different occurrence is preferable, make the inverse choice explicitly and ignore B/C instead; retain exactly one whole occurrence per run.
Build each source partition in ORIGINAL order before arranging final reading order. Quotes must touch: never pair the source ending with an earlier phrase or copy an invalid cut between repeated entries. Return all disjoint ranges, including ignored ones, with no extra copy of already covered text.

{% if includes_footnotes %}
notes array is the single note order. Define each local note_id once: {"operation":"define","note_id":"n","mark":"8"}. mark is the literal printed label, not an internal index or the subject of the note. Use null only when the note has no printed label, not when its topic or reference position is unfamiliar. A numbered annotation about a folio or sheet remains numbered: the leading callout label and the folio/sheet designation in the note body are different things. For example, in "3 Folio XLII, manuscript A", 3 labels the note and XLII identifies the folio; retain mark="3". Its callout may occur before the first word of the section rather than after a sentence. Do not erase the label or leave that leading occurrence as ordinary text merely because the note describes document provenance. A new sentence inside note 7 does not create note 8: in "7 First note. More explanation. 8 Next note.", split immediately BEFORE "8", not before "More explanation". Keep all real notes even when source slicing is difficult.
For the FIRST note appending to the preceding-page note cursor use {"operation":"continue","note_id":"tail"}; there is no mark. Its paragraphs can start anew (join=false) without defining a new note. A note with no available preceding cursor is preserved as an unbound stream. Do not submit a predecessor source, neighbour membership, numeric index or stream_order.
references identify an original layout_id, this SAME note_id and anchor={"before":...,"mark":...,"after":...} from consecutive source text. They annotate a real occurrence in retained paragraph content, not a second copy. Defining notes and assigning their members does NOT create their references: inspect the retained body for each actual printed callout and submit its reference explicitly. A callout enclosed in an OCR formula is still a callout; do not omit it because of that wrapper. Leave references empty only when there is no identifiable real occurrence on this page. Select exactly the printed reference characters, even inside an OCR formula or across formatting nodes. The program splits wrappers and retains every unselected character. Retain every unselected symbol when extracting the number; OCR grouping does not make the whole expression a reference. No extraction flag or special superscript shape is required.

Check ALL retained sources for callouts, not just the first number found inside an OCR formula. Distinct numbered notes may contain identical wording; keep their identities and their respective on-page callouts separate. In symbolic prose, OCR may encode a diagram/emblem glyph and its following callout as a mathematical-looking expression. A TeX operator name is not proof that the adjacent digit is arithmetic: read the surrounding instruction together with the numbered apparatus. Conversely, do not invent references from genuine arithmetic or exponents. Before returning, check each note against its actual occurrences on this page and emit every identifiable callout, including leading callouts and those in other paragraphs. A note whose callout is genuinely absent from this page does not require an invented one.

Example of two separate callouts in symbolic prose: "Make emblem A of \(\star 7\). Make emblem B of \(\times 8\)." The apparatus says "7 A circle containing three dots." and "8 A circle containing three dots." Both 7 AND 8 are references, even though the notes have identical wording and OCR used a TeX operator for the second glyph. Submit both literal ranges linked to their separate note_ids; retain \star and \times as source content. Do not repair only the first occurrence or treat the second as multiplication solely because of its OCR spelling. This differs from an actual arithmetic calculation: determine the printed reading from the prose and apparatus together.

On changing a note, update its record, all members and references together. References target identity, so reordering notes does not redirect them. To retract an erroneously created note, assign its parts their corrected destinations and remove its definition and false references. Do not merely move a reference among members of that same note.
